Great question! 🤔 Let me explain what happens to the frames:

📍 Where Do the Frames Go?
Short Answer: They stay in RAM (memory) temporarily and are never stored on disk.

🔍 Detailed Breakdown:
Server Side:
MSS captures screenshot → Raw pixel data goes to RAM
Data is sent over socket → Copied from RAM through network buffers
After sending → The screenshot data is immediately discarded (garbage collected)
No disk storage
No persistence
Client Side:
Socket receives data → Goes into RAM (receive buffer)
Numpy array created → Lives in RAM
OpenCV displays it → Frame rendered from RAM to screen (GPU framebuffer)
Next iteration → Old frame data is overwritten/garbage collected
No disk storage
Previous frames are lost
💾 Memory Flow Visualization:
Server:
Screen → MSS capture (RAM) → Socket send (RAM) → [Network] → ❌ NOT on disk

Client:
[Network] → Socket receive (RAM) → Numpy array (RAM) → OpenCV display (GPU) → ❌ NOT on disk
⚡ Key Points:
✅ Everything happens in RAM - fast and temporary ✅ No disk I/O - that's why it's real-time ✅ Frames are overwritten each cycle - no accumulation ✅ When program closes - all data is gone (RAM is freed)

🎯 Why This Matters:
Fast: RAM is ~100x faster than disk
No clutter: Your disk doesn't fill up with screenshots
Privacy: No traces left after program exits
Real-time: Low latency streaming possible
